%I A401742 #8 Nov 10 2026 15:19:38
%S A401742 185,2977,6953,36569,66193,83681,215657,267241,581737,982649,1366633,
%T A401742 1230281,1890169,1632737,3837233,4371809,5631433,6662057,6548761,
%U A401742 10269337,8720993,15045529,17738057,20041801,19071713,24290969,29281393,32573801,30832097,33105697
%N A401742 Square roots of the central entries in a six-square 3 X 3 magic-square construction from Pythagorean primes using the semiperimeter and inradius.
%C A401742 For p = A002144(n), let q = A401741(n) and Q = a(n) = p*q.
%C A401742 There are unique integers x > y > 0 such that p = x^2 + y^2. Let u = x^2 - y^2, v = 2*x*y, s = x*(x+y), r = y*(x-y) and C = Q^2. Here u is the odd leg and v is the even leg of the primitive Pythagorean triangle with hypotenuse p, while s and r are its semiperimeter and inradius.
%C A401742 The value q = s^2 + r^2 = p^2 + u*v is the squared radius of the triangle's Conway circle; see A401741 and A088544.
%C A401742 Let X = u*(p*(u+v)-v^2), Y = v*(p*(u+v)+u^2), A = u*(p*(u+v)+v^2), B = v*(u^2-p*(u+v)), d = 2*A*B, e = 2*X*Y, k = (d+e)/2, l = (e-d)/2 and R = u^3 + v^3.
%C A401742 Then the array with rows
%C A401742   [C+k, C-e, C+l],
%C A401742   [C-d, C,   C+d],
%C A401742   [C-l, C+e, C-k]
%C A401742 is a 3 X 3 magic square with magic sum 3*C. The six entries C-e, C-d, C, C+d, C-l and C+e are respectively (X-Y)^2, (A-B)^2, Q^2, (A+B)^2, R^2 and (X+Y)^2.
%C A401742 All nine entries are positive and distinct; see the linked note for the proof. The construction works whether q is prime or composite.
%C A401742 The terms are listed in increasing order of p, not in increasing order of Q; for example, a(11) = 1366633 > a(12) = 1230281.
%C A401742 Since gcd(p,q) = 1, Q is a product of two distinct primes exactly when the corresponding p belongs to A401740.
%C A401742 For background on 3 X 3 magic squares with six square entries, see Morgenstern's discussion of Euler's formulas and Boyer's work linked below.
%H A401742 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A401742/b401742.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..2549</a>
%H A401742 Christian Boyer, <a href="http://www.multimagie.com/English/SquaresOfSquares.htm">Magic squares of squares</a>.
%H A401742 Christian Boyer, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02985794">Some notes on the magic squares of squares problem</a>, The Mathematical Intelligencer, 27 (2005), 52-64.
%H A401742 Christian Boyer, <a href="http://www.multimagie.com/English/SquaresOfSquaresSearch.htm">Latest research on the "3x3 magic square of squares" problem</a>.
%H A401742 Lee Morgenstern, <a href="http://www.multimagie.com/English/Morgenstern14.htm">Euler's Formulas for 3x3 Magic Squares of 6 Squares</a>, December 2011.
%H A401742 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A401742/a401742.txt">Semiperimeter and inradius of Pythagorean triangles and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%H A401742 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A400365/a400365.txt">Fourth powers of Pythagorean legs and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%H A401742 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A401004/a401004.txt">Pythagorean primes, a Pell-type recurrence and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%H A401742 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A401446/a401446.txt">A Fibonacci recurrence step on Pythagorean legs and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%H A401742 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/ConwayCircle.html">Conway Circle</a>.
%F A401742 If p = A002144(n) and s, r are the semiperimeter and inradius of its primitive Pythagorean triangle, then a(n) = p*(s^2+r^2) = p*(p^2+u*v), where u and v are its legs.
%F A401742 a(n) = A002144(n)*A401741(n).
%e A401742 For p = 5, the legs are 3 and 4, so q = 6^2 + 1^2 = 37 and a(1) = 5*37 = 185. The construction gives
%e A401742   [ 28345, 119^2, 60169 ]
%e A401742   [ 257^2, 185^2,  49^2 ]
%e A401742   [  91^2, 233^2, 40105 ]
%e A401742 Every row, column and diagonal sums to 3*185^2 = 102675. All nine entries are positive and distinct, and exactly six of them are squares.
%e A401742 For p = 29, q = 35^2 + 6^2 = 1261 = 13*97 is composite and a(4) = 29*1261 = 36569; the same construction again gives a magic square with six square entries.
%o A401742 (Python)
%o A401742 from math import isqrt
%o A401742 from sympy import primerange
%o A401742 def a401742(limit=400):
%o A401742     # Return terms for hypotenuses p < limit.
%o A401742     out = []
%o A401742     for p in primerange(5, limit):
%o A401742         if p % 4 != 1:
%o A401742             continue
%o A401742         for y in range(1, isqrt(p // 2) + 1):
%o A401742             x = isqrt(p - y*y)
%o A401742             if x*x + y*y == p:
%o A401742                 s, r = x*(x+y), y*(x-y)
%o A401742                 q = s*s + r*r
%o A401742                 out.append(p*q)
%o A401742                 break
%o A401742     return out
%Y A401742 Cf. A002144, A002365, A002366, A068228, A088544, A400363, A400364, A400365, A401002, A401003, A401004, A401444, A401445, A401446, A401740, A401741.
%K A401742 nonn,easy,new
